'''Maracaju''' ({{Lang-ku|Murukuîá}}; {{Literal translation|{{W|passion fruit}}}}) is an [[Communes of Balisca|autonomous commune]] on the island of [[Pindorama]], an [[Indigenous nations in Balisca|Indigenous nation]] and [[communities of Balisca|community]] within [[Balisca]]. It is the most populous city in Pindorama, and the administrative seat of the [[Regions of Balisca|region]] of [[Catanduva]]. It is one of the fastest-growing cities in Balisca, mostly as a result of immigration from [[W:South America|South America]].  

It was founded by [[W:France Antarctique|French colonists fleeing Portuguese Brazil]] in 1567, becoming the colony of ''France Hespérides''. The colonists soon founded a village, [[Sainte-Croix, Maracaju|Sainte-Croix]] ({{Literal translation|holy cross}}). The colony was able to survive due to cordial relations with the local indigenous people and the acceptance of [[Hesperian League|Hesperian suzerainty]] as a bulwark against continued Portuguese attacks. With the arrival of more colonists and traders in 1568, ''France Hespérides'' started to develop, with an economy based mostly in sugar cane production and the [[W:Slave trade|slave trade]].

It sits directly across the [[W:Ilhas Cagarras|Cagarras Strait]] from the [[W:Brazil|Brazilian]] city [[W:Rio de Janeiro|Rio de Janeiro]]. It was administered as part of [[Hesperia]] from 1588 until 1920, when it was returned to the jurisdiction of the Pindorama nation as an autonomous city under the [[W:Indigenous decolonization|Maracaju Accord]].

